  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 304 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry and cool weather is expected today through \nSaturday night as a ridge of surface high pressure brushes by to\nour north. Winds will be gusty out of the north northwest today\nup to around 25 mph at times. Temperatures will also be cool \nenough overnight for some pockets of patchy frost in the coolest\nspots of the Adirondacks and northeast Kingdom. Temperatures \ntoday and Saturday will range through the 60s with Saturday \ncooler than today. Then a warm front will lift into our forecast\narea on Sunday and bring showers to our region, especially our \nsouthern zones. A southward shift in the models with this system\nmeans we are now expecting between a trace and four tenths of \nan inch of rain through Monday morning. Bulk of this system will\nbe south of our region, and slide east and away from the area \nby Monday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A Rex block type of pattern will develop next \nweek to our west, which will hold a ridge of high pressure over \nnorthern New England and Quebec such that we'll get refills of \ncool and dry air. The closed upper level low that gradually \nworks its way eastward through the Great Lakes region may open \nup and get pushed ever so slightly northward as it moves to the \neast, opening the door for increasing rain chances by Thursday. \n\nAside from this potential rain, the only other weather of note \nwill be chilly nights that set the stage for frost development. \nFor now, based on the expected position of high pressure, there \nis a slightly higher chance of some patchy frost in portions of \nthe Adirondacks and central/northeastern Vermont Wednesday \nmorning, but it is on the table each morning Tuesday through \nThursday with relatively light winds each night.",
